# Agent Orchestrator

> **Purpose:** Define workflow-level concerns — command routing, task complexity, MCP usage, and the project adaptation model. Agent and skill selection for domain tasks is handled natively by Cursor via `description` frontmatter fields.

---

## Agent Selection

Cursor selects specialized agents and skills **natively** based on their `description` frontmatter. Each agent in `.cursor/agents/` and each skill in `.cursor/skills/` declares when it should activate. No explicit file-pattern routing is needed — Cursor matches task context to agent/skill descriptions dynamically.

When delegating to a domain specialist, check if `project-config.json` exists in the skill directory to pass project-specific context.

**One agent is dispatched explicitly, not natively:** `codebase-analyzer` is the per-component research subagent used by `/initialize` (Phases 4, 5 Part 1.5, 6) and is always invoked through the `Task` tool with `subagent_type: "codebase-analyzer"`, one call per component, all calls in a single message. It is the only agent in this template allowed to write files from inside a subagent, and its write scope is bounded by the brief — the `Task` tool has **no** `readonly` parameter (that field belongs to `.cursor/agents/` frontmatter). For ordinary code search outside initialization, use the built-in `explore` subagent. Full contract: [`subagent-contract.md`](mdc:../skills/project-initialization/references/subagent-contract.md).

---

## Workplace Documentation (upstream)

The AI Workplace’s own user-facing guides live under `docs/**` in the **template repo** (initialization, roles, APM, customization, daily workflow, TDD, update/rollback, architecture). **They are not copied into initialized projects**, so never link or read them as project-local paths. The map (which document answers what, when to read it, key anchors) and the local-first → Git-URL resolution contract are in [`workplace-docs.md`](mdc:../skills/project-initialization/references/workplace-docs.md); `scripts/fetch-workplace-doc.js` resolves any of them. Consult it whenever the user asks how the Workplace itself works, or when you need a current Workplace procedure that is not in `.cursor/`.

